<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Deliberately committed, and deliberately almost empty.

  @bacons/apple-targets generates this file during prebuild — but only when it
  is absent (with-widget.js: `if (!fs.existsSync(filePath))`). What it generates
  for a watch target is literally `<dict/>`, because everything else comes from
  INFOPLIST_KEY_* build settings. Committing our own version is therefore the
  supported way to add a key, and nothing is lost by doing it.

  CFBundleIconName is the one key that has to be here. The plugin does produce
  the icon correctly — targets/watch/Assets.xcassets/AppIcon.appiconset ends up
  holding a 1024x1024 watchOS entry, and ASSETCATALOG_COMPILER_APPICON_NAME is
  set to AppIcon on the watch target — but the built app's Info.plist still
  comes out without CFBundleIconName, and App Store Connect refuses the upload:

      Validation failed (409) Missing Icons. No icons found for watch
      application 'Tee.app/Watch/TeeWatch.app'.

      Validation failed (409) Missing Info.plist value. A value for the
      Info.plist key 'CFBundleIconName' is missing in the bundle
      'com.teegolf.app.watchkitapp'.

  That check happens on Apple's servers, after a full build and upload. Setting
  the key here is what stops it. Keep this file minimal: anything the plugin's
  build settings already supply would collide if repeated.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleIconName</key>
	<string>AppIcon</string>
</dict>
</plist>
